{"name":"Cardamom Green","hex":"#989057","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cardamom","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An herb, native to India (Elettaria cardamomum).","Seed of Elettaria cardamomum, used as a medicine and spice, especially in curry powder."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cardamom"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cardamom","blocks":[{"text":"Cardamom, sometimes cardamon or cardamum, is a spice made from the seeds of several plants in the genera Elettaria and Amomum in the family Zingiberaceae. Both genera are native to the Indian subcontinent and Indonesia. They are recognized by their small seed pods: triangular in cross-section and spindle-shaped, with a thin, papery outer shell and small, black seeds; Elettaria pods are light green and smaller, while Amomum pods are dark brown and larger."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/02017_0119_Kardamom%2C_Winter_in_den_Beskiden.jpg/330px-02017_0119_Kardamom%2C_Winter_in_den_Beskiden.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cardamom"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Elettaria_cardamomum_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-057.jpg/330px-Elettaria_cardamomum_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-057.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Elettaria_cardamomum_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-057.jpg/330px-Elettaria_cardamomum_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-057.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"True cardamom plant (Elettaria cardamomum)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AElettaria_cardamomum_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-057.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Cardamom_Seeds_BNC.jpg/500px-Cardamom_Seeds_BNC.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Cardamom_Seeds_BNC.jpg/1280px-Cardamom_Seeds_BNC.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Cardamom_Seeds_BNC.jpg/500px-Cardamom_Seeds_BNC